# Synopsis

Read a file, or list the entries of a directory. File content is
returned with 1-based line numbers; directory entries are listed one
per line. Reading is read-only and needs no approval.

# Result layout

The result is a header line followed by the content (the requested
lines or entries). The header is always present and is always the
first line. It has the form:

    <file type>: <filename>[ <detail>]

- <file type> names the kind of path; it is one of the labels under
  "File types".
- <filename> is the pathname as given, possibly in shell-quoted form
  (see "Name escaping").
- <detail> reports the range of lines or entries shown, or the reason
  no content follows.

# File types

The header for each kind of path. Only a regular file and a directory
have content lines after the header.

- regular file: <path>, lines X-Y of N
- regular file: <path>, empty
- regular file: <path>, offset N is beyond the end (M lines)
- regular file: <path>, contains a NUL byte, likely a binary file; content not shown
- directory: <path>, entries X-Y of N
- directory: <path>, empty
- directory: <path>, offset N is beyond the end (M entries)
- symbolic link: <path> -> <target>
- character device: <path>
- block device: <path>
- FIFO: <path>
- socket: <path>
- unreadable file: <path>
- no such file or directory: <path>
- unknown file type: <path>

"unreadable file" reports a path that exists but cannot be read (for
example, permission denied). "unknown file type" reports a file type
other than the ones listed, such as a Solaris door or a BSD whiteout;
these occur only on some systems.

# Reading a file

For a text file, each content line is a line of the file prefixed
with its 1-based line number, a colon, and a space ("<number>: ").
The number is its shortest non-empty decimal form (no leading zeros):

    regular file: src/main.c, lines 1-3 of 42
    1: #include <stdio.h>
    2:
    3: int main(void) {

The content is shown losslessly: every line appears exactly as it is
in the file, with only the line-number prefix added. The one
exception is the final newline: when the last line has no trailing
newline, the header ends with ", no trailing newline" (the displayed
last line is still newline-terminated):

    regular file: notes.txt, lines 1-2 of 2, no trailing newline
    1: hello
    2: world

A file containing a NUL byte is treated as binary and its content is
not shown:

    regular file: image.bin, contains a NUL byte, likely a binary file; content not shown

# Listing a directory

Each entry is listed by name with a marker for its type: a regular
file is shown by name alone, a directory ends in "/", a symbolic link
shows its target, and a special file shows its type in parentheses:

    directory: src, entries 1-5 of 5
    lib/
    main.c
    Makefile
    link -> lib/main.c
    pipe (FIFO)

# Name escaping

A pathname, a symbolic link target, and each entry name in a
directory listing, is shown shell-quoted (in the $'...' form) when it
contains any of the characters : , > $ ( ) " ' , a whitespace
character, or a control character; otherwise it is shown as-is.
Inside the $'...' form the escapes \\, \', \a, \b, \f, \n, \r, \t and
\v are decoded, and any other character is literal. A name that is not
wrapped in $'...' is literal.

# Notes

- offset (default 1) is the first line or entry to read, limit
  (default 1500) the maximum number to read. Read a further page by
  raising offset.
- Use the reported line numbers to refer to specific lines when
  editing.
